OCRINOPATÍAS:Endocrinopathies are disorders related to the endocrine system that can arise from immune checkpoint inhibitors.", "CHECKPOINT:", "EMA:The European Medicines Agency (EMA) is an agency of the European Union tasked with the evaluation, supervision, and safety monitoring of medicinal products. It plays a crucial role in ensuring the safety and efficacy of medicines available in the EU. The EMA has been involved in the review of various medicinal products, including obesity treatments such as lorcaserin, which it did not approve for marketing due to safety concerns. Additionally, the agency has rejected certain obesity treatments, including fentermina and topiramato, based on risk assessments. Overall, the EMA is dedicated to maintaining high standards for medicinal products within the European Union, focusing on the health and safety of the public.", "LYSODREN:Lysodren is a medication with the active ingredient mitotane, used in the treatment of adrenal cancer.", "YERVOY:Yervoy is a medication with the active ingredient ipilimumab, used in cancer immunotherapy.", "MITOTANE:Mitotane is a drug used to treat adrenal cancer by inhibiting adrenal cortex function.", "ZARZUELO A:Zarzuelo A is an author who contributed to the book on pharmacological interactions.", "FLUDROCORTISONA:Fludrocortisona, also known as fludrocortisone, is a synthetic corticosteroid medication primarily used in the treatment of isolated aldosterone deficiency and adrenal insufficiency. It plays a crucial role in managing conditions related to insufficient production of aldosterone, helping to regulate sodium and potassium levels in the body.", "HYPERURICEMIA:Hyperuricemia is a medical condition characterized by elevated levels of uric acid in the blood. This condition can lead to various health issues, most notably gout, and is often influenced by dietary factors, including purine intake and alcohol consumption. Hyperuricemia is relevant in the context of dietary management for conditions such as nephrolithiasis and can frequently occur in patients. It may also be a complication associated with Glycogen Storage Disease I (GSDI). Treatment for hyperuricemia typically involves dietary modifications and medical intervention to manage uric acid levels effectively."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "temperature": 0.0, "frequency_penalty": null, "presence_penalty": null, "top_p": 1.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1}, "history": null}